errno: the E* names carry the LV2 status codes on the PS3 LV2 target

LV2 system calls return 0x8001xxxx status codes.  Code written for the
platform compares those returns against the E* names (ENOMEM, EIO, EBUSY,
...) and expects errno to hold the same values, but newlib defined the POSIX
numbers, so the comparisons never matched and errno and the system-call
returns disagreed.  Add <sys/_lv2_errno.h> with the 61 LV2 codes and include
it at the end of <sys/errno.h> when __lv2ppu__ is defined; names that are not
LV2 codes keep newlib's values.  librt's lv2error() becomes an identity map
for these codes.  An ABI change: objects built against the old values compare
against the wrong numbers, so it ships with the other v0.20.0 ABI changes.

--- a/newlib/libc/include/sys/errno.h
+++ b/newlib/libc/include/sys/errno.h
@@ -192,6 +192,11 @@
 
 #define __ELASTERROR 2000	/* Users can add values starting here */
 
+#ifdef __lv2ppu__
+/* PS3 LV2: errno holds the LV2 status codes (see <sys/_lv2_errno.h>).  */
+#include <sys/_lv2_errno.h>
+#endif
+
 #ifdef __cplusplus
 }
 #endif
--- a/newlib/libc/include/sys/_lv2_errno.h
+++ b/newlib/libc/include/sys/_lv2_errno.h
@@ -0,0 +1,134 @@
+/* <sys/_lv2_errno.h>: errno values for the PS3 LV2 target.
+
+   LV2 system calls return 0x8001xxxx status codes, and errno holds the
+   same codes, so a code returned by a system call compares equal to the
+   E* name for it.  Included at the end of <sys/errno.h> when __lv2ppu__
+   is defined; names that are not LV2 codes keep newlib's values.  */
